 return a message prompting to upgrade NexLev subscription.\n82: \n83: **Step 6 — Verify NexLev is active in chat**\n84: \n85: Before starting, confirm NexLev is enabled in your Claude chat window. Click the **+** button in the message bar, hover over **Connectors**, and check that the toggle next to **NexLev** is turned on.\n86: \n87: ![Verify NexLev connector is enabled in Claude chat](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/images/documentation/mcp/claude-nexlev-permission.png)\n88: \n89: > **Info**\n90: >\n91: > **No API key needed.** Claude.ai uses OAuth to connect directly to your NexLev\n92: > account. Works on web and mobile — your tools sync across all devices.\n93: \n94: [Connect on Claude →](https://claude.ai/customize/connectors)\n95: \n96: #### Claude Code CLI\n97: \n98: ##### Via Claude Code CLI\n99: \n100: > **Warning**\n101: >\n102: > Requires the **Claude Code CLI**. If you don't have it yet, install it first:\n103: > ```bash npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code ``` Verify with `claude\n104: > --version`.\n105: \n106: **Step 1 — Active NexLev account**\n107: \n108: Make sure your NexLev account is active before proceeding.\n109: \n110: **Step 2 — Add the NexLev MCP server**\n111: \n112: Run this once from any terminal:\n113: \n114: ```bash\n115: claude mcp add -s user --transport http nexlev https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp\n116: ```\n117: \n118: | Field | Value |\n119: | --- | --- |\n120: | Name | `nexlev` |\n121: | Server URL | `https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp` |\n122: | Transport | `HTTP` |\n123: | Scope | `user — available in all projects on your machine` |\n124: \n125: The `-s user` flag registers the server globally so it's available in every project. Omit it (or use `-s local`) to restrict it to the current directory only.\n126: \n127: **Step 3 — Authenticate**\n128: \n129: 1. Start Claude Code in any directory:\n130:    ```bash\n131:    claude\n132:    ```\n133: 2. Run the `/mcp` slash command inside the Claude prompt:\n134:    ```\n135:    /mcp\n136:    ```\n137: 3. Select **nexlev** from the list, then choose **Authenticate**.\n138: 4. Your browser opens to `dashboard.nexlev.io` — log in with your NexLev account and approve the connection.\n139: \n140: > **Warning**\n141: >\n142: > **Sign in with the right NexLev account.** If you have a paid NexLev plan\n143: > (Lite, Pro, or Premium), make sure you log in with that account — your rate\n144: > limits are tied to the account you connect. Signing in with a different\n145: > account will apply Free plan limits instead.\n146: \n147: 5. Back in the terminal you'll see:\n148:    ```\n149:    Authentication successful. Connected to nexlev.\n150:    ```\n151: \n152: **Step 4 — Verify the connection**\n153: \n154: ```bash\n155: claude mcp list\n156: ```\n157: \n158: You should see:\n159: \n160: ```\n161: nexlev: https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp (HTTP) - ✓ Connected\n162: ```\n163: \n164: **Step 5 — Use it**\n165: \n166: Just talk to Claude in natural language — it picks the right NexLev tool automatically:\n167: \n168: ```\n169: Find 10 faceless YouTube channels in the personal finance niche earning over $5K/month\n170: ```\n171: \n172: ```\n173: Pull the full analytics for @MrBeast — subscriber growth, RPM, and top videos\n174: ```\n175: \n176: > **Info**\n177: >\n178: > **Token stored locally.** Your OAuth token lives in `~/.claude.json` — treat\n179: > it like a credential and don't commit it. It persists across sessions\n180: > automatically. If it ever expires, re-run `/mcp` → select `nexlev` →\n181: > **Authenticate** to refresh.\n182: \n183: **Managing the server**\n184: \n185: | Task                          | Command                                                          |\n186: | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n187: | List all MCP servers          | `claude mcp list`                                                |\n188: | Inspect NexLev's config       | `claude mcp get nexlev`                                          |\n189: | Remove the NexLev server      | `claude mcp remove nexlev`                                       |\n190: | Re-authenticate (token issue) | Inside `claude`, run `/mcp` → select `nexlev` → **Authenticate** |\n191: \n192: If `claude mcp list` shows `✗ Failed to connect`, the server URL is unreachable. Remove and re-add it with the correct URL.\n193: \n194: **Troubleshooting**\n195: \n196: **`! Needs authentication` after running `/mcp`**\n197: The browser flow didn't complete. Re-run `/mcp` and make sure you finish the login on `dashboard.nexlev.io` until the browser tab shows a success page.\n198: \n199: **`✗ Failed to connect`**\n200: \n201: - Confirm the URL is correct: `https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp`\n202: - Check your network / VPN / firewall — the CLI must reach `*.nexlev.io` over HTTPS\n203: - Run `curl -I https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp` to test reachability\n204: \n205: **Tool call returns `access_denied` or `rate_limited`**\n206: \n207: - `access_denied` — your NexLev plan doesn't include this tool. [Upgrade your plan](?required_nexlev_lite_pro_access=true) in the dashboard.\n208: - `rate_limited` — you've hit your plan's daily quota. Wait and retry, or upgrade.\n209: \n210: **Claude doesn't seem to use NexLev tools**\n211: Run `/mcp` to confirm `nexlev` is **Connected**. If you added it with `local` scope, you must launch `claude` from that project directory. Re-add with `-s user` to make it global.\n212: \n213: ### ChatGPT\n214: \n215: #### ChatGPT Web/Desktop\n216: \n217: ##### Via ChatGPT custom MCP app (OAuth)\n218: \n219: **Step 1 — Active NexLev account**\n220: \n221: Make sure your NexLev account is active before proceeding.\n222: \n223: **Step 2 — Enable Developer mode**\n224: \n225: Go to **chatgpt.com → Settings → Apps → Advanced settings** and toggle on **Developer mode**.\n226: \n227: [Connect on ChatGPT](https://chatgpt.com/#settings/Connectors)\n228: \n229: ![Enable Developer mode in ChatGPT Advanced settings](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/images/documentation/mcp/chatgpt-step-1-developer-mode.png)\n230: \n231: **Step 3 — Create a new app**\n232: \n233: Go to **Settings → Apps → Create App**.\n234: \n235: **Step 4 — Enter the app details**\n236: \n237: Fill in the following information exactly as shown:\n238: \n239: | Field | Value |\n240: | --- | --- |\n241: | Name | `NexLev` |\n242: | MCP Server URL | `https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/mcp` |\n243: | Authentication | `OAuth` |\n244: \n245: ![Create App in ChatGPT Settings](https://dashboard.nexlev.io/images/documentation/mcp/chatgpt-step-2-create-app.png)\n246: \n247: **Step 5 — Confirm and connect**\n248: \n249: Tick **\"I understand and want to continue\"**, then click **Create**. ChatGPT will auto-discover the OAuth settings — no Client ID or secret needed.\n250: \n251: When you first use a NexLev tool, ChatGPT will redirect you to sign in with your NexLev account. Click **Approve** to connect.\n252: \n253: > **Warning**\n254: >\n255: > **Sign in with the right NexLev account.** If you have a paid NexLev plan\n256: > (Lite, Pro, or Premium), make sure you log in with that account — your rate\n257: > limits are tied to the account you connect. Signing in with a different\n258: > account will apply Free plan limits instead.\n259: \n260: > **Info**\n261: >\n262: > **No API key needed.** ChatGPT uses OAuth to connect directly to your NexLev\n263: > account. You can use the same NexLev account across both ChatGPT and Claude —\n264: > each gets its own connection.\n265: \n266: #### Codex CLI\n267: \n268: ##### Via Codex CLI\n269: \n270: > **Warning**\n271: >\n272: > Requires the **Codex CLI**. If you don't have it yet, install it first:\n273: > ```bash npm install -g @openai/codex ``` Verify with `codex --version`.\n274: \n275: **Step 1 — Active NexLev account**\n276: \n277: Make sure your NexLev account is active before proceeding.\n278: \n279: **Step 2 — Add the NexLev MCP server**\n280: \n281: Run this once from any terminal:\n282: \n283: ```bash\n284: codex mcp add nexlev --url https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/codex-mcp\n285: ```\n286: \n287: | Field | Value |\n288: | --- | --- |\n289: | Name | `nexlev` |\n290: | Server URL | `https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/codex-mcp` |\n291: | Transport | `Streamable HTTP` |\n292: \n293: This registers the server globally in `~/.codex/config.toml`, available across all projects.\n294: \n295: **Step 3 — Authenticate**\n296: \n297: 1. Start Codex in any directory:\n298:    ```bash\n299:    codex\n300:    ```\n301: 2. Run the `/mcp` slash command inside the Codex prompt:\n302:    ```\n303:    /mcp\n304:    ```\n305: 3. Select **nexlev** from the list, then choose **Authenticate**.\n306: 4. Your browser opens — log in with your NexLev account and approve the connection.\n307: \n308: > **Warning**\n309: >\n310: > **Sign in with the right NexLev account.** If you have a paid NexLev plan\n311: > (Lite, Pro, or Premium), make sure you log in with that account — your rate\n312: > limits are tied to the account you connect. Signing in with a different\n313: > account will apply Free plan limits instead.\n314: \n315: **Step 4 — Verify the connection**\n316: \n317: ```bash\n318: codex mcp list\n319: ```\n320: \n321: You should see `nexlev` listed as connected.\n322: \n323: **Step 5 — (Optional) Manual config edit**\n324: \n325: Alternatively, you can directly edit `~/.codex/config.toml` and add:\n326: \n327: ```toml\n328: [mcp_servers.nexlev]\n329: url = \"https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/codex-mcp\"\n330: ```\n331: \n332: > **Info**\n333: >\n334: > **Config shared across CLI and IDE.** Once you set up the server in\n335: > `~/.codex/config.toml`, it works across both the Codex CLI and IDE extension\n336: > without redoing setup.\n337: \n338: **Managing the server**\n339: \n340: | Task                 | Command                                                         |\n341: | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n342: | List all MCP servers | `codex mcp list`                                                |\n343: | Remove NexLev        | `codex mcp remove nexlev`                                       |\n344: | Re-authenticate      | Inside `codex`, run `/mcp` → select `nexlev` → **Authenticate** |\n345: \n346: ### OpenClaw\n347: \n348: #### Via OpenClaw\n349: \n350: > **Info**\n351: >\n352: > **How it works.** OpenClaw doesn't talk to NexLev directly — it routes tasks\n353: > through **Claude Code CLI** or **Codex CLI** already installed on your machine\n354: > via its ACP (Agent Client Protocol). The flow is: **You (messaging app) →\n355: > OpenClaw Gateway → Claude Code / Codex CLI → NexLev MCP tools**\n356: \n357: **Prerequisites — CLI installed & NexLev registered**\n358: \n359: Before setting up OpenClaw, confirm at least one CLI is installed and NexLev MCP is already registered in it — OpenClaw calls the CLI, which in turn calls NexLev.\n360: \n361: Register NexLev in **Claude Code CLI** (if using Claude):\n362: \n363: ```bash\n364: claude mcp add -s user --transport http nexlev https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp\n365: ```\n366: \n367: Register NexLev in **Codex CLI** (if using Codex):\n368: \n369: ```bash\n370: codex mcp add nexlev --url https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/mcp\n371: ```\n372: \n373: See the **Claude** or **ChatGPT** tabs above for full CLI setup instructions.\n374: \n375: ---\n376: \n377: **Step 1 — Install OpenClaw**\n378: \n379: ```bash\n380: npm install -g openclaw@latest\n381: openclaw onboard --install-daemon\n382: ```\n383: \n384: This installs the Gateway daemon and walks you through pairing with your messaging channel (Telegram, Discord, WhatsApp, etc.).\n385: \n386: ---\n387: \n388: **Step 2 — Install the acpx plugin**\n389: \n390: ACP sessions are powered by the `acpx` plugin. Install and enable it:\n391: \n392: ```bash\n393: openclaw plugins install @openclaw/acpx\n394: openclaw config set plugins.entries.acpx.enabled true\n395: openclaw config set plugins.allow '[\"acpx\"]'\n396: ```\n397: \n398: Then enable ACP and set your default CLI:\n399: \n400: ```bash\n401: openclaw config set acp.enabled true\n402: openclaw config set acp.backend acpx\n403: openclaw config set acp.allowedAgents '[\"claude\", \"codex\"]'\n404: \n405: # Pick your default (whichever CLI you have installed):\n406: openclaw config set acp.defaultAgent claude\n407: # or:\n408: openclaw config set acp.defaultAgent codex\n409: ```\n410: \n411: ---\n412: \n413: **Step 3 — Register NexLev inside OpenClaw**\n414: \n415: The URL depends on which CLI OpenClaw will call:\n416: \n417: ```bash\n418: # If your default CLI is Claude Code:\n419: openclaw mcp set nexlev '{\"url\":\"https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp\",\"transport\":\"streamable-http\"}'\n420: \n421: # If your default CLI is Codex:\n422: openclaw mcp set nexlev '{\"url\":\"https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/mcp\",\"transport\":\"streamable-http\"}'\n423: ```\n424: \n425: Verify it was saved:\n426: \n427: ```bash\n428: openclaw mcp list\n429: ```\n430: \n431: > **Note**\n432: >\n433: > **Prefer manual config?** Edit `~/.openclaw/openclaw.json` directly and add\n434: > your server entries under `mcp.servers`, then run `openclaw gateway restart`.\n435: \n436: ---\n437: \n438: **Step 4 — Authenticate NexLev**\n439: \n440: OpenClaw inherits auth from the CLI it calls. Authenticate inside that CLI:\n441: \n442: ```bash\n443: claude    # or: codex\n444: /mcp\n445: # Select nexlev → Authenticate\n446: # Browser opens → log in to dashboard.nexlev.io → approve\n447: ```\n448: \n449: > **Warning**\n450: >\n451: > **Sign in with the right NexLev account.** If you have a paid NexLev plan\n452: > (Lite, Pro, or Premium), make sure you log in with that account — your rate\n453: > limits are tied to the account you connect. Signing in with a different\n454: > account will apply Free plan limits instead.\n455: \n456: ---\n457: \n458: **Step 5 — Spawn a session & use NexLev**\n459: \n460: From your connected messaging app (Telegram, Discord, etc.), spawn a session:\n461: \n462: ```\n463: /acp spawn claude --bind here\n464: ```\n465: \n466: The `--bind here` flag pins the conversation to the session so follow-up messages route to the same CLI instance. Then just ask in natural language:\n467: \n468: ```\n469: Find 10 faceless YouTube channels in the finance niche earning over $5K/month\n470: ```\n471: \n472: ```\n473: Pull full analytics for @MrBeast — subscriber growth, RPM, and top videos\n474: ```\n475: \n476: ---\n477: \n478: **Managing the server**\n479: \n480: | Task                         | Command                     |\n481: | ---------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n482: | List MCP servers in OpenClaw | `openclaw mcp list`         |\n483: | Remove NexLev from OpenClaw  | `openclaw mcp unset nexlev` |\n484: | Check ACP health             | `/acp doctor` (in chat)     |\n485: | Check active sessions        | `/acp status` (in chat)     |\n486: | Close a session              | `/acp close` (in chat)      |\n487: | Restart the gateway          | `openclaw gateway restart`  |\n488: \n489: **Troubleshooting**\n490: \n491: **NexLev tools not visible**\n492: OpenClaw calls the CLI — the CLI is what talks to NexLev. Verify the CLI itself has NexLev registered: `claude mcp list` or `codex mcp list`.\n493: \n494: **Wrong endpoint**\n495: Using the Claude Code URL with Codex (or vice versa) will silently fail.\n496: \n497: - Claude Code → `https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp`\n498: - Codex → `https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/mcp`\n499: \n500: **ACP spawn blocked**\n501: ACP sessions run on the host runtime. If your session is sandboxed, use `runtime=\"subagent\"` or disable sandboxing for the session.\n502: \n503: ### Hermes\n504: \n505: #### Via Hermes Agent\n506: \n507: > **Info**\n508: >\n509: > **How it works.** Hermes is a self-hosted autonomous agent that connects\n510: > natively to NexLev as an MCP server — no intermediary CLI required. The flow\n511: > is: **You (messaging app) → Hermes Gateway → NexLev MCP tools**\n512: >\n513: > Unlike the OpenClaw path (which routes through Claude Code / Codex CLI),\n514: > Hermes talks to MCP servers directly via its built-in MCP client.\n515: >\n516: \n517: **Prerequisites — Hermes installed**\n518: \n519: **macOS / Windows (recommended):** Download the Hermes Desktop installer from [hermes-agent.nousresearch.com](https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com) — it installs both the desktop app and the CLI in one step.\n520: \n521: **Linux / macOS / WSL2 (CLI only):**\n522: \n523: ```bash\n524: curl -fsSL https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/install.sh | bash\n525: source ~/.bashrc   # or source ~/.zshrc\n526: ```\n527: \n528: ---\n529: \n530: **Step 1 — Run first-time setup**\n531: \n532: ```bash\n533: hermes setup\n534: ```\n535: \n536: Walks you through choosing a model provider (Nous Portal, OpenRouter, Anthropic, etc.). If you already have `~/.openclaw`, the setup wizard detects it and offers to migrate your settings, memories, and API keys automatically.\n537: \n538: ---\n539: \n540: **Step 2 — Register NexLev as an MCP server**\n541: \n542: Add NexLev to `~/.hermes/config.yaml`:\n543: \n544: ```yaml\n545: mcp_servers:\n546:   nexlev:\n547:     url: \"https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp\"\n548:     auth: oauth\n549: ```\n550: \n551: Verify it was saved:\n552: \n553: ```bash\n554: hermes mcp list\n555: ```\n556: \n557: Test the connection:\n558: \n559: ```bash\n560: hermes mcp test nexlev\n561: ```\n562: \n563: ---\n564: \n565: **Step 3 — Authenticate NexLev**\n566: \n567: On first connection, run:\n568: \n569: ```bash\n570: hermes mcp login nexlev\n571: ```\n572: \n573: A browser opens → log in to `dashboard.nexlev.io` → approve. Tokens are cached at `~/.hermes/mcp-tokens/nexlev.json` and reused silently on subsequent runs.\n574: \n575: > **Warning**\n576: >\n577: > **Sign in with the right NexLev account.** If you have a paid NexLev plan\n578: > (Lite, Pro, or Premium), make sure you log in with that account — your rate\n579: > limits are tied to the account you connect. Signing in with a different\n580: > account will apply Free plan limits instead.\n581: \n582: > **Note**\n583: >\n584: > **On a remote / headless host?** When Hermes runs on a VPS, the loopback OAuth\n585: > callback can't reach your browser. Hermes will print an authorize URL and a\n586: > paste-back prompt. Open the URL in your browser, approve, copy the full\n587: > redirect URL (a connection error on that page is expected), and paste it at\n588: > the prompt.\n589: \n590: ---\n591: \n592: **Step 4 — Connect a messaging platform (e.g. Telegram)**\n593: \n594: Interactive setup (recommended):\n595: \n596: ```bash\n597: hermes gateway setup\n598: ```\n599: \n600: Select **Telegram** when prompted. The wizard asks for your bot token and allowed user IDs and writes the config automatically.\n601: \n602: Manual setup — add credentials to `~/.hermes/.env`:\n603: \n604: ```bash\n605: TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456789:ABCdefGHIjklMNOpqrSTUvwxYZ\n606: TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USERS=123456789    # comma-separated for multiple users\n607: ```\n608: \n609: > **Note**\n610: >\n611: > **Don't have a bot token?** Create one via **@BotFather** (`/newbot`). Get\n612: > your numeric user ID via **@userinfobot**.\n613: \n614: Then start the gateway in the foreground:\n615: \n616: ```bash\n617: hermes gateway\n618: ```\n619: \n620: Or, if you've installed it as a background service:\n621: \n622: ```bash\n623: hermes gateway start\n624: ```\n625: \n626: ---\n627: \n628: **Step 5 — Use NexLev from your messaging app**\n629: \n630: Message your bot in natural language:\n631: \n632: ```\n633: Find 10 faceless YouTube channels in the finance niche earning over $5K/month\n634: ```\n635: \n636: ```\n637: Pull full analytics for @MrBeast — subscriber growth, RPM, and top videos\n638: ```\n639: \n640: ---\n641: \n642: **Managing the server**\n643: \n644: | Task                            | Command                       |\n645: | ------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n646: | List MCP servers                | `hermes mcp list`             |\n647: | Remove NexLev                   | `hermes mcp remove nexlev`    |\n648: | Test NexLev connection          | `hermes mcp test nexlev`      |\n649: | Re-authenticate NexLev          | `hermes mcp login nexlev`     |\n650: | Re-select NexLev tools          | `hermes mcp configure nexlev` |\n651: | Check gateway service status    | `hermes gateway status`       |\n652: | Restart the gateway service     | `hermes gateway restart`      |\n653: | Check platform status (in chat) | `/platforms`                  |\n654: | Reload MCP config (in chat)     | `/reload-mcp`                 |\n655: \n656: **Troubleshooting**\n657: \n658: **NexLev tools not visible**\n659: Run `hermes mcp list` to confirm the entry exists, then `hermes mcp test nexlev` to verify it's reachable. If the connection succeeded but tools are missing, run `hermes mcp configure nexlev` to check your tool selection checklist.\n660: \n661: **Wrong endpoint**\n662: Hermes uses the Claude-MCP endpoint. Make sure your config has:\n663: `https://prod.dashboard.nexlev.io/api/claude-mcp`\n664: Not the Codex/plain variant (`/api/mcp`).\n665: \n666: **Auth expired**\n667: Run `hermes mcp login nexlev` to refresh the OAuth token.\n668: \n669: **Gateway not responding**\n670: Check with `hermes gateway status`. Restart with `hermes gateway restart`.\n671: \n672: > **Info**\n673: >\n674: > **Coming from OpenClaw?** Run `hermes claw migrate` to import your settings,\n675: > memories, skills, and API keys in one step. The setup wizard also auto-detects\n676: > `~/.openclaw` if you run `hermes setup` fresh.\n677: \n678: ## What You Can Do Next\n679: \n680: Once connected, start using NexLev tools directly inside your AI chat:\n681: \n682: ### Long Form Channel Search\n683: \n684: ```\n685: Find 20 long-form channels related to the minecraft niche\n686: ```\n687: \n688: ### Short Form Channel Analysis\n689: \n690: ```\n691: Show me 15 viral YouTube Shorts channels in the comedy niche\n692: ```\n693: \n694: ### Keyword Research\n695: \n696: ```\n697: Find high-volume, low-competition keywords for cooking content\n698: ```\n699: \n700: ### Content Strategy\n701: \n702: ```\n703: What content strategy should I use for a tech review channel with 10K subscribers?\n704: ```\n705: \n706: ### Competitive Research\n707: \n708: ```\n709: Compare successful channels in the fitness niche and identify common patterns\n710: ```\n711: \n712: ### Thumbnail Generation\n713: \n714: ```\n715: Generate a thumbnail for my video \"I Tried Waking Up at 4AM for 30 Days\" — match the style of the top productivity channels\n716: ```\n717: \n718: ### Off-Platform Earnings\n719: \n720: ```\n721: What does @AliAbdaal sell outside of ads, and which brands sponsor the most finance channels?\n722: ```\n723: \n724: ### Network Research\n725: \n726: ```\n727: Which network is MrBeast signed to, and what other channels are under it?\n728: ```\n729: \n730: ## Understanding NexLev Responses\n731: \n732: When you use NexLev through Claude, ChatGPT, or Claude Code CLI, you'll receive interactive widgets displaying:\n733: \n734: - **Channel data** — Comprehensive metrics, performance indicators, and growth analytics\n735: - **Sortable results** — Click column headers to sort by different metrics\n736: - **Expandable details** — View in-depth information for each result\n737: - **Filtering options** — Refine results by various criteria\n738: - **Pagination** — Navigate through large result sets\n739: \n740: > **Tip**\n741: >\n742: > **Pro Tip:** Results are interactive! You can sort, filter, and expand\n743: > channels directly within the chat interface for deeper analysis.\n744: \n745: ## Rate Limits\n746: \n747: All limits are **per user, per tool, and reset every 24 hours**.\n748: \n749: > **Note**\n750: >\n751: > Lite and Premium share identical rate limits across all tools. If you're on\n752: > the Premium plan, the numbers in the Lite column apply to you.\n753: \n754: ### Networks & CMS\n755: \n756: > **Note**\n757: >\n758: > CMS tools are available on **Lite, Pro, and Premium** plans only — Free plan\n759: > users do not have access.\n760: \n761: | Tool | Free | Lite | Pro |\n762: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n763: | Content owner lookup (`get_content_owner`) | 0 | 5 | 15 |\n764: | CMS network search (`search_cms_networks`) | 0 | 5 | 15 |\n765: \n766: With **usage credits** enabled, calls past the daily limit cost $0.05 each for both tools. See [CMS Networks](/docs/mcp/cms-networks).\n767: \n768: ### Channel Discovery\n769: \n770: | Tool | Free | Lite | Pro |\n771: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n772: | Long-form channel search (`find_long_form_channels`) | 10 | 150 | 300 |\n773: | Short-form channel search (`find_shorts_channels`) | 10 | 150 | 300 |\n774: | Similar channels (`get_similar_channels`) | 5 | 20 | 30 |\n775: | Latest discovered faceless niches (`latest_discovered_faceless_niches`) | 20 | 150 | 300 |\n776: | Faceless channel check (`check_faceless_channel`) | 5 | 100 | 300 |\n777: | Channel resolver (`channel_resolver`) | 10 | 100 | 500 |\n778: | Niche channel search (`search_niche_finder_channels`) | 10 | 100 | 200 |\n779: \n780: ### Video Intelligence\n781: \n782: | Tool | Free | Lite | Pro |\n783: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n784: | Similar videos (`get_similar_videos`) | 5 | 20 | 30 |\n785: | Video search (`search_videos`) | 10 | 150 | 300 |\n786: | YouTube search (`youtube_search`) | 50 | 100 | 500 |\n787: | Video details (`youtube_video_details`) | 10 | 100 | 300 |\n788: | Video comments (`youtube_video_comments`) | 10 | 150 | 300 |\n789: | Video transcript (`get_video_transcript`) | 5 | 150 | 300 |\n790: | Bulk video transcripts (`get_bulk_video_transcripts`) | 5 | 50 | 100 |\n791: | Video subtitle (`get_video_subtitle`) | 5 | 150 | 300 |\n792: | Bulk video subtitles (`get_bulk_video_subtitles`) | 5 | 50 | 100 |\n793: | Channel outlier videos (`youtube_channel_outliers`) | 10 | 50 | 100 |\n794: | YouTube suggested videos (`search_youtube_suggested_videos`) | 20 | 150 | 300 |\n795: | Viral videos — small channels (`search_viral_videos_small_channels`) | 20 | 150 | 300 |\n796: | Faceless outlier videos (`faceless_outliers_videos`) | 20 | 150 | 300 |\n797: \n798: ### Visual Search\n799: \n800: | Tool | Free | Lite | Pro |\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
